// Subject: the game's environment destruction and damage model, game state only.
// The global substance registry — the numeric half of the material library.
//
// The Blender side is `materials.blend` next to this file: one material datablock per entry,
// LINKED (never appended) into each tank's .blend and assigned to its ballistic meshes. The
// datablock NAME is the join key; this file owns every scalar (design doc §12: no numbers in
// the model, §13.7: substance is authored per-plate, paint is a runtime livery).
//
// Every entry is one of three KINDS. `Hard(..)` is matter (ADR-0040): a layer of the §13 walk that
// resists the shell, is charged its spend and shadows the spall field. `Witness` is not matter: it
// states no resistance (none, not zero), never enters the walk, and takes dose from what the hard
// world sends through it — the shell's line at the ROUND's crew line rate (`wound`, a round file's
// field), each spall direction at its strength. A witness substance sits only on a crew volume and a
// crew volume wears only a witness substance; the bake refuses a tank file that breaks either.
// `Unworn(..)` is worn by no mesh: it states only facts a round reads and nothing the walk reads,
// so the walk refuses a volume that names it.
//
// `burst` = a burst core's facts, the two a round's burst block reads when it names this entry as
// its `core_substance` (`density`, kg/m³, and `speed_constant_mps`, the speed constant K in m/s,
// the sideways speed scale of the burst's v_s). An optional field: a `Hard(..)` entry may state it
// beside its resistance, and an `Unworn(..)` entry states it alone.
//
// `resistance` = how the substance resists a round's force law. Exactly one entry is `Reference`:
// the reference steel, whose `facts` are the force law's reference constants and one metre of which
// is 1000 reference-mm by definition. Every other entry declares `Effective(factor)`: reference-mm
// per metre of chord (the §13.2 field value), i.e. it resists as `factor / 1000` of the reference
// force at every speed. Declared, never inferred from facts or from a fill fraction.
//
// `facts` = the material facts of a structural metal (density kg/m³, flow strength Pa at large
// strain, Young's modulus Pa, Poisson's ratio). `None` where no row is sourced; non-metals
// (ammunition, rubber, engine block) never carry facts. Facts on an `Effective` entry are recorded
// for the mechanisms that will read them; the force law does not read them yet, and the strength
// ratio they give (DERIVED, at the strength-limited end) differs from the declared factor:
//   - GunSteel, 4340-class quenched-and-tempered analogue: a / a_ref ≈ 1.129 (≈ 1129), ρ / ρ_ref ≈ 0.994.
//   - MildSteel, 1020 analogue: a / a_ref ≈ 0.460 (≈ 460), ρ / ρ_ref ≈ 1.006.
//   - TungstenCarbide, 6 % cobalt cemented-carbide analogue: a / a_ref ≈ 4.16 (≈ 4160),
//     ρ / ρ_ref ≈ 1.898.
//   - CastMild, ASTM A27 grade 65-35 analogue: a / a_ref ≈ 0.385 (≈ 385), ρ / ρ_ref ≈ 0.994.
//   - TrackSteel, ASTM A128 (Hadfield) analogue: a / a_ref ≈ 0.673 (≈ 673), ρ / ρ_ref ≈ 1.005.
//   Cast armour has no sourced row.
//
// `bulk` = the bulk facts of a non-metal (density kg/m³ and optionally a seam between a
// construction's pre-cut chunks: `tensile`, its tensile strength, Pa, and
// `fracture_energy`, its mode-I fracture energy G_f, J/m², stated together): the construction bake
// reads the density for each chunk's mass; the constructions' fracture laws read the density, the
// factor and the seam, and an element of a substance stating no seam never breaks.
// Optional, absent read as `None`, and never stated beside `facts` (each states a density).
// A breakable seam also requires `shear_cohesion` (c, Pa, without precompression) and `shear_fracture_energy` (cohesive G_II, J/m², excluding
// continuing friction work). All four seam facts are stated together or none is; an incomplete
// breakable row is refused at load. The punch ring reads the shear facts. A breakable row also
// states `bulk.blast`: compressive strength (Pa), dynamic/static compressive and tensile ratios,
// fully dense grain/cell-wall density (kg/m³) and Young's modulus (Pa). The crush law reads the
// compression, its ratio and the fully dense density; the scab law reads the modulus and tensile
// ratio. Reference strain rates live in the ratios' provenance. Missing or unusable inputs
// are refused at load.
// Sources and analogue limits: .agents/scratch/blast-b3-material-sources.md. A non-metal's
// effective factor is its compressive (crushing) strength over the reference flow strength
// (~1 GPa), × 1000: a strength ratio, the reading the steels' DERIVED rows note, not a measured
// penetration equivalence.
//
// `debris_half_angle_deg` = the half-angle α (degrees) of the spall cone a body exit through this
// substance throws, the rim of its debris field: a constant of the material, not of the exit's
// speed. Converted once at parse to the field law's ratio λ = 1/sin α (design doc §5); refused
// outside (0°, 90°].
//
// PROVENANCE: every number is authored `(value, Kind("source or reason"))`, Kind names the value's job
// (`src/provenance.rs`); a number without one does not load.
// "Q2" below is the research table in
// `.agents/scratch/damage-cam-2026-10-08/research/hardness-anchor-free.md`. "Research pass
// 2026-08-07" is the Tiger metallurgy pass whose sources live in that session's record, not in the
// repo. `visual` is a key and the reference's resistance the unit's definition; neither is a number
// with a provenance.
//
// Effective factors: baseline carried from the per-node values in tiger_1.tank.ron (2026-08-06); revised by the
// material research pass 2026-08-07 (Tiger metallurgy, sources in the session record):
// DOCTRINE (Yan ruling 2026-08-07): factors are PHYSICS-HONEST, never bent for gameplay.
// Feel belongs to the laws and the hp/damage model, not to material constants. (Sibling of the
// VFX honesty doctrine.)
// THE PRINCIPLE (research pass 2026-08-07): a factor tracks ALLOY AND HEAT TREATMENT, never the
// FORMING PROCESS. "It was cast" says nothing ballistic on its own — what resists a shell is what
// the steel is made of and what was done to it afterwards. Casting, rolling and forging only matter
// insofar as they correlate with those two, and on German wartime steels they correlate WEAKLY:
// the same process spans armor and machinery grades. So a new substance is earned by a different
// alloy/treatment, not by a different way of shaping it.
//   - Tiger armor = rolled homogeneous nickel-steel, NOT face-hardened -> RHA everywhere, no
//     FaceHardened substance needed for this tank.
//   - `Cast` = 900 — cast ARMOR (Panzerguß) specifically, NOT "anything cast". German wartime
//     practice names `Panzer-Stahlguß` (armor-grade cast steel, alloyed and heat-treated to an
//     acceptance spec) and plain `Stahlguß` (ordinary cast steel, machinery) as DISTINCT GRADES
//     with distinct acceptance requirements — Spielberger reproduces the acceptance specs, and they
//     are not the same document. Eliding the two promotes every machinery casting on the vehicle to
//     armor, which is how a cast gearbox housing ends up resisting like a mantlet. Cast armor ran
//     ~5-15% below rolled ballistically; 900 is the mid of that range. If it is cast and it is NOT
//     armor-grade, it is `MildSteel` — see the note there.
//   - `GunSteel` unified at 1000 (was 800): forged autofrettaged gun steel >= RHA quality;
//     the old barrel-1000/breech-800 split was unresearched gameplay residue, and gun-part
//     fragility is the hp/damage model's job. The breech hardened 800->1000 when the material
//     bind landed (2026-08-07) — this table is what it reads now.
//   - `MildSteel` = 800: NON-ARMOUR structural and machinery steel — WROUGHT OR CAST. The name is
//     the one weak spot in this table: it reads like a forming process and is not one. It covers
//     the stamped/welded wheel bodies and forged rims, and equally the CAST running gear (sprockets
//     and idlers, 2026-08-07) — a sprocket is plain `Stahlguß`, machinery steel that happens to be
//     cast, so it is NOT `Cast` (see the principle above). At ~0.8x RHA. A cast machinery part
//     belongs in `CastMild` below; the model's sprockets and idlers still wear `MildSteel` until a
//     source settles how they were made (`.agents/docs/design/tank-surfaces.md` §4).
//     The wheels softened 1000->800 when the material bind landed; running-gear shots were a real
//     weak axis. Rename to `Structural` if the name ever misleads someone into the `Cast` mistake
//     this note exists to prevent.
//   - `CastMild` = 800: plain cast machinery steel (Stahlguß): sprockets, idlers, housings. Its own
//     row because its facts are a casting grade's, not a wrought one's, and its surface looks cast;
//     its declared factor is `MildSteel`'s class, so moving a part here changes nothing ballistic
//     until a law reads the facts. Still NOT `Cast`: that is armour-grade.
//   - `TrackSteel`: cast track-link steel, the Hadfield manganese class (ASSUMED; no source states
//     a given vehicle's track alloy). Worn by no mesh yet: the track links draw through their own
//     belt material. A tank sheet's `track.substance` names it: its friction is the track's side
//     of every pair, the belt on the ground or a piece, and its bump stops and side plates.
//   - `EngineBlock` = 150: homogenized box — grey cast iron ~0.4x RHA, ~30-40% metal fraction
//     along a shot line -> 0.4 x 0.35 ~= 0.14. HL210 fork (first ~250 Tigers, aluminium
//     block) would sit ~100; declare the vehicle's date to settle it. Transmission shares
//     the substance for now (denser gear mass — split only if the sandbox ever cares).
//
// `visual` = the key of this material's look in the view's shared visual library (`visuals.ron`
// beside this file), which also decides whether the livery's paint goes on it. Every hard row names
// one, and many rows share one: cast armour and cast machinery steel both look like cast steel. The
// server parses it as a key and nothing more. Design: `.agents/docs/design/tank-surfaces.md`.
//
// THIS FILE IS THE CLASSIFIER. A glTF primitive is a ballistic volume iff its material name is a
// key here, and the bake refuses one wearing an `Unworn` key; the tank RON carries no per-node
// material factor. Adding a key
// makes every mesh wearing that material armour — and removing one silently un-declares them. What
// SHOWS that is `L1.SOURCE_CENSUS`, which prints per-substance primitive counts against the
// previous committed source: evidence for a human to read, never a pass condition. No test pins
// those counts, because a second vehicle is a different model and not a broken one.
(
    substances: {
        "RHA": Hard(
            resistance: Reference,
            visual: "rolled_steel",
            feedback: (
                exposed_linear_rgb: ((0.5, 0.51, 0.53), Taste("material contact and fracture effects: ASSUMED: provisional exposed-surface and powder read; no measured optical response read for this substance")),
                powder_linear_rgb: ((0.47, 0.47, 0.47), Taste("material contact and fracture effects: ASSUMED: provisional exposed-surface and powder read; no measured optical response read for this substance")),
                metallic: (1.0, Taste("material contact and fracture effects: ASSUMED: provisional exposed-surface and powder read; no measured optical response read for this substance")),
                roughness_gain: (1.0, Taste("material contact and fracture effects: ASSUMED: provisional exposed-surface and powder read; no measured optical response read for this substance")),
                sparks: (true, Taste("material contact and fracture effects: ASSUMED: provisional contact-spark emission for this substance")),
